# Pre-commit hook — lint only staged files (~3-5s)
# Blocks commit on lint errors. Type check + native import check run in pre-push.
#
# 2026-10-05 flow-selector gate (stage 3): a staged flow using an id: that is
# not in maestro/tools/testid-map.json would ship a selector that can never
# resolve on device, and would NOT be caught by tsc/lint (YAML is not linted).
#
# `testid-map-freshness` job of .github/workflows/ci.yml — the SAME discovery
# (git ls-files) and the SAME `node maestro/tools/testid-map-freshness.cjs`,
# so a stale map blocks HERE before it ever reaches CI. Runs on EVERY commit,
# not only map commits: both maps are regenerated from the STAGED worktree.
#
# `check:i18n-orphans` job (npm run check:i18n-orphans) + HMR `prune` job.
#
# `shellcheck` job of .github/workflows/ci.yml — the SAME discovery (git
# ls-files '*.sh' 'scripts/git-hooks/*') and the SAME `shellcheck -x`,
# so a shell-script warning blocks HERE before it ever reaches CI. Runs on
# EVERY commit, not only shell commits: `-x` follows `# shellcheck source=`
# links, so an edit to a sourced script can break a script this commit never
# staged — a staged-only trigger would let that through. ~1s for the whole set.
#
# Stage-8 RATchet: any disable NOT in scripts/shellcheck-disable-baseline.json,
# and in any file that shellcheck -x reports a finding for, BLOCKS the commit.
# The baseline may only SHRINK: a commit may remove a disable from a file, and
# that deletion deletes the baseline entry for that file (the baseline is a
# set of file-to-disable-key pairs, not a free-form allow-list). It may never
# add a disable to the baseline on its own authority: a disable must first be
# removed from the file it currently carries.
# Stage 8 ratchet: disable files, purge, and re-check.
# Vanilla shellcheck -x treats every # shellcheck disable=X line as user-side
# overrides that silently nullify real findings (including a finding in a
# script this commit never touched: a sourced file downstream can carry an old
# disable for a rule that now fires there). So this stage treats the disable
# set as a RATchet:
# 1. PURGE: delete every # shellcheck disable line whose key set is NOT in
#    scripts/shellcheck-disable-baseline.json; if the file keeps no disables,
#    delete the line entirely.
# 2. CHECK: shellcheck -x again; any remaining disable that also fires BLOCKS
#    the commit (the gate refuses to let a disabled finding survive the ratchet).
# 3. RATSET: add newly-validated disables to the baseline (never the reverse),
#    then re-check the baseline against the file set.
# Discovery: the same git ls-files set stage 7 uses, so the baseline stays in
